CORE
JmlListIterableCheckcast
--function "JmlListIterableCheckcast.useIt:()Ljava/lang/Iterable;" --axiomatic-collections --unwind 3 -cp target/classes
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR
^.*FAILURE$
--
Regression for the JDK-known-subtype augmentation in
remove_instanceof.cpp::subtype_expr.

Background
----------
JBMC lowers a Java `instanceof T` expression to a
disjunction of `@class_identifier == "java::C"` checks over
every loaded subclass of T. The list comes from
class_hierarchyt::get_children_trans(target).

When the JDK's collection types are loaded as bare stubs
(no `.class` body parsed — only the type's name is in the
symbol table, with `struct C { @java.lang.Object; }`), the
stub doesn't declare its super-interfaces. So the class
hierarchy doesn't see `java.util.List` as a child of
`java.lang.Iterable`, and an `(Iterable)someList` checkcast
fails the disjunction.

Fix
---
add_jdk_known_subtypes() in remove_instanceof.cpp augments
the children list with a hardcoded set of well-known JDK
collection edges (List ⊆ Collection ⊆ Iterable, etc.).
The list is conservative — only public-API
relationships that don't vary across JDK versions.

Without the fix this test fails with a Dynamic cast check
on the `return l;` statement (an implicit (Iterable)
upcast). With the fix it passes cleanly.
